\section*{Minimum Distinct Distances Among Planar Point Sets}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
\textit{Almost all planar pins}: For any fixed positive $\varepsilon$, the claimed number of pins determining fewer than $n^{1-\varepsilon}$ nonzero distances is $o(n)$ uniformly over planar $n$-point sets. This is an almost-all-pins conclusion rather than an exact linear lower bound at every pin.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Mathematical claim 1.} For finite $P\subset\mathbb R^2$ put $D_x(P)=\{|y-x|:y\in P\setminus\{x\}\}$ and $k_P(x,y)=|\{z\in P\setminus\{x\}:|z-x|=|y-x|\}|$. The claim states that for every $s>0$, \[\sup_{|P|=n}\frac{|\{(x,y)\in P^2:x\ne y,\ k_P(x,y)\ge n^s\}|}{n(n-1)}\longrightarrow0.\] Consequently, for every $\epsilon>0$, $\sup_{|P|=n}|\{x:|D_x(P)|<n^{1-\epsilon}\}|/n\to0$. Thus all but $o(n)$ pins determine at least $n^{1-\epsilon}$ distances, uniformly over the configuration; the sharp $n/\sqrt{\log n}$ global scale is not asserted.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
\textit{Almost all planar pins}: An extremal pair graph is extracted from large distance fibers while retaining nonconcentration within those fibers. Real-closed-field transfer moves finite configurations into number fields without altering their distance relations. Factorization into two complex coordinates and the product formula balance local closeness partitions across absolute values, giving an incompatibility with the assumed abundance of large fibers.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
For every fixed $\varepsilon>0$, the claimed almost-all-pins theorem implies at least $n^{1-\varepsilon}$ distinct distances in every sufficiently large planar $n$-point set. It improves a lower bound for the quantity asked about, while leaving its exact value and sharp asymptotic order undetermined.

\subsection{Formal verification}
Family 167 describes Lean coverage for the weak pinned-distance conclusion and the separate fixed-power unit-distance improvement.

\textit{Almost all planar pins}: The manuscript links \texttt{PinnedDistances.lean} (declaration \texttt{OAI.WeakPinned.main}), \texttt{PlanarUnitDistances.lean}.

The documented selection includes this manuscript’s main assertion; that assertion retains the restrictions stated above.

This site has not run the Lean development. These links describe OpenAI’s selected formal statements and should be read with their stated scope.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI reports that an internal model produced the manuscripts. Their arguments have not been independently verified as proofs by this site. Any portion outside the documented formal scope may contain mistakes, and the catalogue label reports the announced mathematical claim.

